{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nOperator directive (verbatim, 2026-08-13): 'One source of contention for CI runners in this project is the reconciler bridge job and canary. let's double the time between runs for both jobs and for both fallback and the chained reconciler invocation.'\n\nThe operator names the reconciler bridge job and canary as a source of CI-runner contention on navapbc's shared GitHub Free pool (20 concurrent jobs org-wide). Attribution must be stated honestly: dotted-uncheery-dunnart measured the 2026-08-12/13 run mix as 609 `main|workflow_dispatch` (the Gerrit Verified lane, one dispatch per patchset) and 169 `main|schedule`, and that Verified-lane starvation is a SEPARATE open ticket this change does not address. The bridge and canary sit in the 169 scheduled runs, plus a near-continuous dispatch stream whenever the opt-in `RECONCILE_CONTINUOUS` chain is enabled. Halving every bridge/canary lever cuts that share of the pool; it is a reduction in this pair's footprint, not a fix for the Verified lane.\n\n**Honest cost of lever 2.** The paced chain sleeps inside its own job: it halves chained runs and dispatch events but does not release the runner slot during the pause. Freeing the slot outright means turning `RECONCILE_CONTINUOUS` off — a repo Variable, not a code change, and out of scope.\n\n## Approach\nFour cadence levers, all doubled:\n\n1. **Bridge cron backstop** — `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` `schedule:` goes from `*/20 * * * *` to a **uniform 40-minute** cadence. Cron cannot express 40 min in one entry, so use the three-entry 2-hour cycle GitHub accepts under one `schedule:` key: `0 0-23/2 * * *`, `40 0-23/2 * * *`, `20 1-23/2 * * *` (fires :00, :40, 1:20, 2:00, … — gaps uniformly 40 min).\n2. **Chain pacing** — the \"Re-dispatch to sustain the continuous loop (opt-in)\" step calls `gh workflow run` immediately, so the chain's inter-invocation period equals exactly **one** pass duration (~7 min here, per `docs/jira-sync-setup.md`). No delay knob exists. Add one that is self-tuning rather than a repo-specific literal, since this file is a client-copied TEMPLATE: a new early step records `PASS_STARTED_EPOCH` into `$GITHUB_ENV`, and the re-dispatch step sleeps for the pass's own elapsed duration before dispatching. Period becomes **two** pass durations — doubled at any pass duration, for any adopter. Three plain-literal bounds, no new repo Variable:\n   - **Unset guard.** The step runs under `set -euo pipefail`, and `test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py::_run_redispatch` runs the step body in isolation with `env={PATH, MODE}` only — no `PASS_STARTED_EPOCH`. So the step reads `started=\"${PASS_STARTED_EPOCH:-}\"` and, when empty or not all-digits, **skips pacing entirely** (prints a note, dispatches immediately). Arithmetic never sees an empty string, `-u` cannot abort the step, and the disabled-422 test passes unchanged.\n   - **Cap.** The sleep is capped at a literal **900 s (15 min)**.\n   - **Skip threshold.** Pacing is skipped once elapsed >= 1800 s (30 min): such a pass already paces itself, and the remaining `timeout-minutes: 60` budget must stay free.\n   Pacing lives inside the step's existing `RECONCILE_CONTINUOUS` gate, so the template default (chain off) is unaffected.\n3. **Canary schedule** — `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge-canary.yml` `0 * * * *` (hourly) -> `0 */2 * * *` (2-hourly).\n4. **Canary staleness window** — the canary header states the window rationale as \"3x cadence\"; at 40 min that floor rises 1h -> 2h, equalling today's default window, so leaving it would alarm on healthy-but-late runs. Double the default `ALERT_WINDOW_HOURS` 2 -> 4 (workflow `env:` default plus the `alert_window_hours` input default/description), preserving the same 2x margin. `scripts/canary_bridge.py` reads the value from the environment with no hard-coded default, so only its ticket-body string \"(cron: */20)\" changes.\n\n**Jenkins parity.** `Jenkinsfile` spells the backstop `cron('H/20 * * * *')`. Jenkins `H/40` gives non-uniform 40/20 gaps, so mirror the three-entry cycle as a multi-line `cron('''...''')`. `.gitlab-ci.yml` has no cron (GitLab schedules are server-side) — no edit.\n\n**Truthfulness.** Every comment naming `*/20` or \"hourly\" is rewritten: `reconcile-bridge.yml` header + re-dispatch comments and `::warning::` text; the canary header staleness paragraph; `scripts/canary_bridge.py`'s alert body; `docs/jira-sync-setup.md`'s cadence table, its \"…than the 20-minute floor\" heading (plus its three in-repo anchor links), its bullets and the exit-75 row; the `test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py` docstring.\n\n**Latency note.** Worst-case reconcile latency doubles (20 -> 40 min) and canary detection latency doubles (1h -> 2h poll, 2h -> 4h window). Recorded in `docs/jira-sync-setup.md` beside the cadence table — the authoritative cadence home. `docs/ci-provider-matrix.md` covers the external LLM suite's provider cadence, not the bridge, so it is deliberately untouched.\n\n## Scope\n- `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` — schedule, new pass-start step, paced re-dispatch, comments.\n- `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge-canary.yml` — schedule, `ALERT_WINDOW_HOURS` defaults, header.\n- `Jenkinsfile` — mirrored 40-minute backstop.\n- `scripts/canary_bridge.py` — alert-body cadence string.\n- `docs/jira-sync-setup.md` — cadence table, continuous-loop section + anchors, latency note.\n- `tests/unit/test_ci_tickets_push_policy.py` — `_UNCHANGED_CRONS` pin + uniformity assertion.\n- `tests/unit/test_ci_main_push_policy.py` — `test_reconcilers_keep_their_own_schedules_and_never_cancel`.\n- `tests/unit/workflow/test_bridge_provider_wrappers_heldout.py` — `test_github_schedule_and_continuous_redispatch_remain_compatible`.\n- `tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py` — docstring + paced re-dispatch branches.\n\n## Testing\n- **Happy path:** the three cadence pins named in Scope each compare against the full three-entry list.\n- **Uniformity:** a new assertion in `tests/unit/test_ci_tickets_push_policy.py` derives the fire times of the three cron entries over one 2-hour cycle and asserts every consecutive gap is exactly 40 minutes — the property the three-entry spelling exists to satisfy, which a literal-only pin would not catch.\n- **Edge / failure path:** extend `_run_redispatch` in `tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py` to exercise (a) a short elapsed time (sleep taken, cap not hit), (b) elapsed past the 1800 s skip threshold (no sleep, dispatch still issued), and (c) `PASS_STARTED_EPOCH` absent — the harness's existing default env — asserting exit 0, no sleep, and a dispatch. (a)/(b) stub `sleep` on `PATH` so the test records the requested duration instead of waiting. The pre-existing disabled-workflow-422 benign-no-op test must still pass unchanged; case (c) is exactly its guard.\n- **Gates:** `make lint`, `make typecheck`, the actionlint/zizmor workflow gates, and the CI unit selection.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] `reconcile-bridge.yml` `schedule:` holds exactly `0 0-23/2 * * *`, `40 0-23/2 * * *`, `20 1-23/2 * * *`, and a test asserts consecutive fire-time gaps are uniformly 40 minutes.\n- [x] `reconcile-bridge-canary.yml` schedule is `0 */2 * * *` and its `ALERT_WINDOW_HOURS` default (env + input) is `4`.\n- [x] The re-dispatch step sleeps for the pass's own elapsed duration before dispatching, capped at a literal 900 s, skipped above 1800 s elapsed, and skipped when `PASS_STARTED_EPOCH` is unset or non-numeric (no new repo Variable); tests assert the sleeping branch, the elapsed-skip branch, and the unset-guard branch.\n- [x] `Jenkinsfile` carries the same three-entry 40-minute backstop.\n- [x] `grep -rn '\\*/20' .github/workflows/ Jenkinsfile scripts/ docs/jira-sync-setup.md` returns no hits, and no comment or doc still calls the canary \"hourly\" or the window 2h.\n- [x] The tests named in Scope pass against the new literals, and `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the CI unit selection are green."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786621632034122001,"uuid":"cb6b41c2-9e3b-45de-a00b-a88583f20904"}